  "description": "Removal of glazing and reinstatement of external balcony.\r\rThis application seeks Listed Building Consent for the removal of a later, non-original enclosure to the south-facing balcony of Flat 253, Lauderdale Tower, Barbican Estate (Grade II listed), in order to reinstate the balcony to its original open form.\r\rThe balcony was enclosed in 2001 (City of London Planning Ref: BAR II/22), prior to the listing of the Barbican Estate. The enclosure introduced hardwood-framed windows, secondary joinery and flashing above the original cast concrete parapet, converting the balcony into internal accommodation. The original living room windows and sliding balcony access door were retained and remain unaltered.\r\rThe current proposal involves careful removal of the later glazing, timber framing, alcove joinery, flashing, and floor coverings. The original cast concrete balcony floor panels and concrete surfaces will be retained, exposed and gently cleaned. Any making-good to exposed concrete is expected to be minimal and will be undertaken using restrained methods and materials consistent with the existing concrete. The overall approach will be one of minimal intervention, allowing the re-exposed concrete surfaces to naturally reintegrate visually over time rather than introducing conspicuous new finishes or surface treatments. No alterations are proposed to the original windows, sliding door, balcony parapet, soffit, or structural concrete frame.\r\rThe balcony enclosure is set back within the parapet, and removal works will be undertaken by suitably qualified contractors using controlled dismantling methods and appropriate safety measures.\r\rThe proposal removes non-original fabric and restores the balcony?s intended external function, fa?ade depth, and architectural expression, resulting in a beneficial heritage impact."
